r cancer cells. This places Surgical Staging as the central decision-enabling entity in the community. [Data: Entities (2198); Relationships (3793, 3790)]\\n\\nThe workflow is explicitly linked to specimen acquisition (“samples”) and downstream testing for cancer cells, tying procedural steps to diagnostic outputs that can be used intraoperatively and/or postoperatively to assign stage and guide subsequent treatment decisions. [Data: Entities (2198, 2218); Relationships (3793, 3790)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Detecting microscopic metastases is a key rationale for surgical staging, especially in ovarian cancer contexts\",\"explanation\":\"Microscopic metastases—spread not visible grossly or on imaging—are explicitly connected to Surgical Staging as something the procedure is designed to detect via sampling and microscopic evaluation. This highlights why surgical staging can materially alter staging determinations when disease is not otherwise apparent. [Data: Entities (2186, 2198); Relationships (3755)]\\n\\nThe community’s description emphasizes that surgical staging can detect microscopic disease by collecting tissue and/or fluid specimens for examination, reinforcing that its value comes from pathology-confirmed findings rather than visual inspection alone. [Data: Entities (2198, 2218, 2186); Relationships (3793, 3755)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Lymph node evaluation is central: pelvic and para-aortic nodes are primary targets in cervical cancer staging pathways\",\"explanation\":\"Pelvic lymph nodes are presented as clinically important in gynecologic cancers—particularly cervical cancer—because metastatic involvement is a defining feature of advanced staging categories. They may be surgically removed or sampled during related procedures and are commonly evaluated as part of staging to determine extent of disease. [Data: Entities (4718); Relationships (10600, 10245)]\\n\\nPara-aortic lymph nodes are described as abdominal lymph nodes relevant to evaluation and management; they can be surgically removed (sometimes in addition to pelvic nodes) as part of certain surgeries or surgical staging procedures, and their status can influence treatment planning. [Data: Entities (4719, 2198); Relationships (10540)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Stage 3C cervical cancer is directly characterized by nodal involvement, linking staging categories to pelvic lymph node status\",\"explanation\":\"Stage 3C cervical cancer is defined in the dataset as a FIGO stage category characterized by cancer in pelvic lymph nodes near the cervix and/or para-aortic lymph nodes, indicating that nodal status is not merely prognostic but constitutive of stage assignment. [Data: Entities (4717, 4718, 4719)]\\n\\nA specific relationship ties Stage 3C cervical cancer to pelvic lymph nodes, reinforcing the rule-based dependency of this stage on pelvic nodal involvement. [Data: Relationships (10215); Entities (4717, 4718)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Surgical staging commonly includes omentum removal and systematic sampling to assess common spread sites\",\"explanation\":\"The omentum is explicitly linked to Surgical Staging as an anatomic structure removed during staging to assess spread sites, matching the broader description that ovarian cancer surgical staging often includes omentectomy as part of systematic evaluation. [Data: Entities (2203, 2198); Relationships (3791)]\\n\\nSurgical Staging is also described as involving the taking of samples from organs/tissues (including fluid or washings) to test for cancer cells and determine stage, underscoring a systematic, multi-site assessment rather than a single-biopsy approach. [Data: Entities (2218, 2198); Relationships (3793, 3790)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Procedural pathways for pelvic node removal appear in multiple contexts (dissection and trachelectomy)\",\"explanation\":\"Pelvic lymph node dissection is explicitly defined as the surgical removal of pelvic lymph nodes and is tied to planned-surgery recommendations, indicating a standardized procedural